00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.819 align:start position:0%
 
we can speak now - Jamie Margolin  founder of the student-led zero-hour  movement which aims to train young  people and promote young climate change  activists she joins us live from Seattle  hello to you Jamie you started  campaigning on climate change at the age  of fourteen I wonder what was the  trigger for you what was it that made  you think I need to stop I need to do  something to change this well for my  generation there has never been a time  in my life where climate change wasn't  looming over me there was never been a  time in my life where it wasn't  dictating every decision that I made  because as a young person we're  constantly being asked to plan our  future what are you going to be when you  grow up what do you want to do and it's  very hard to plan for that in an  environment and a planet that you don't  know if it's gonna be livable so around  when I was 14 years old is when the 2016  election results came in and Donald  Trump was elected president and that was  kind of the final straw for me to take  action because now we have a full-blown  climate denier and a majority in the  United States government that even  denies the facts of this issue so for me  it's always been urgent to act on  climate change but the catalyst was  really the 2016 election that was just  like you know what my leaders are  putting profits over my life they'd  rather line their money lined their  pockets with the money of the fossil  fuel industry then make sure that their  children can breathe yes you say you  live in a country where the president  has ripped up climate change policy  vowed to pull out of the Paris agreement  do you feel that you're fighting a  losing battle there well there's no  choice but to fight this battle it's as  if there's a ticking time bomb that  needs to be stopped and there's not a  very high chance of us stopping it but  we have to stop it or at least try  because otherwise it's going to blow up  in our faces and destroy everything and  so for me I it's not a matter of you  can't really weigh the odds too much  when you're fighting for survival you  just have to you just have to fight for  your generation survival I don't really  have anything to plan forward to live  for if the earth that I'm living on can  sustained civilization as we know it  today and right now maybe the president  and the highest office is our denying  climate change but there's so much  grassroots work being done like with my  organization and through our there are  so many young people taking action  there are so many organizations and  local governments that are determined to  take action that we just have to fight  it by any means necessary and through  all the angles that we can with zero  hour we do mobilizations we're  organizing a Youth Climate Action summit  in Miami Florida this summer we do  marches we also tackle I also tackle  this from the legal perspective I'm on a  lawsuit suing my state for better  climate laws and so you you just have to  attack this issue by Eddie  by all means necessary and by all sorts  of strategies because it's kind of a our  survival is on the line and it's an  emergency and we're acting like it so I  have to fight like it's an emergency  like I'm fighting for my generations  lives because that's really what's  happening it's not just another issue on  the list not just another thing to be  concerned about its tied with everything  President Trump has put the economy  there at the heart of his presidency in  the heart of his campaign for  re-election and it's almost as if it's  the economy versus the environment in  the environment isn't going to win in  votes well that's false because first of  all how are you gonna have a functioning  economy in an unlivable planet with it's  just it's if you just think of it in  business which is it's bad business to  destroy life on Earth how are you going  to have business if you have  catastrophic climate disasters if you  have all the terrible effects of climate  change the uncertainty the instability  the violence that comes out of plunging  resources it's just simply bad business  plus it's proven that renewable energy  is a much more profitable market and  it's it's coming up to be a lot cheaper  than fossil fuels and it just it doesn't  make sense to say that we should  sacrifice all life on Earth for the  temporary wealth of businesses because  they don't want to change first of all  not only is that cruel and inhumane but  it's just not true it's not a choice  between  the environment or business it's either  we go down this road of just greed and  kill ourselves or we improve our planet  and improve businesses with newer  cleaner and better jobs because let's be  honest no one actually enjoys working in  a coal mine no one actually enjoys that  that is very dirty and hard work and so  renewable energy jobs are significantly  better so it just it's just completely  false and it's that narrative is really  just propaganda from the fossil fuel  industry as a scare tactic saying that  we can't save life on earth and we can't  care for people or animals or the  environment because they just want to  get rich and they're currently getting  rich off of oil gas and coal and they're  scared to change but it's not a choice  between the environment and the economy  that is one of the biggest myths ever  Jamie before we go I just want to ask  you about the situation in Oregon where  Republican senators have walked out of a  debate on the climate bill and looking  to limit carbon emissions by 2050 what  does that tell us about the state of  debate well there's there's just so much  climate denial right now in the United  States that it's it's very concerning  because a lot of times like these  politicians those who are walking out  those who are just completely closing  their eyes to the climate crisis these  politicians are really in the pockets of  the fossil fuel industry they get most  of their a lot of big chunks of their  campaign money from the fossil fuel  industry and so in turn they owe them  favors and so even though a lot of these  politicians I think personally know  better and personally know that the  climate crisis is a huge threat that  it's tied with everything and that it's  threatening life on Earth they are  pressured by these industries that are  funding their campaigns to do favors for  them so they will go out and vote  against policies knock down policies  obstruct any climate progress not  because they don't know better but  because they're there in the pockets of  these industries and until we can get  those industries out of our politics  it's going to be very hard to take  really urgent the radical action that we  need because  right now at least in the United States  and I know this isn't unique to the  United States a lot of politicians are  in the pockets of these industries and  kind of doing their bidding so that  means that they're not actually  representing the constituents the people  that they're supposed to be helping a  government's job is to protect young  people like me I'm only 17 years old I  in theory have a long and prosperous  life to look forward to but that's the  government's job to protect kids like me  and to protect people like me who do not  have any power but are just stuck in  this system and right now they are  choosing to they're choosing to put  profits over my life and the life of  children and it manifests into denialism  like that

